WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.963
Accuracy, and why it isn’t enough.

2
00:00:02.243 --> 00:00:08.557
Put the expert’s labels and the judge’s verdicts in a two-by-two table, with pass as the positive class.

3
00:00:08.837 --> 00:00:19.760
True positives are passes the judge called pass; false negatives are passes it called fail; false positives are failures it let through; true negatives are failures it caught.

4
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.389
Accuracy is the share of all examples on which they agree.

5
00:00:23.669 --> 00:00:27.659
It’s the number people report, and on skewed data it misleads badly.

6
00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:30.541
Suppose 90 percent of your outputs are fine.

7
00:00:30.821 --> 00:00:39.547
A “judge” that says pass to everything scores 90 percent accuracy while catching zero failures, which are the only thing you built it to find.

8
00:00:39.827 --> 00:00:49.960
So report the two conditional rates separately, as Hamel recommends: True positive rate (TPR): of the outputs the expert passed, the share the judge passed.

9
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.779
TP divided by (TP plus FN).

10
00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:58.456
True negative rate (TNR): of the outputs the expert failed, the share the judge failed.

11
00:00:58.736 --> 00:01:01.147
TN divided by (TN plus FP).

12
00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:04.093
These answer two different operational questions.

13
00:01:04.373 --> 00:01:09.088
Low TNR means bad outputs slip through, so the eval overstates quality.

14
00:01:09.368 --> 00:01:16.152
Low TPR means good outputs get flagged, so the eval understates quality and your reviewers waste time.

15
00:01:16.432 --> 00:01:19.696
Which one you care about more depends on what the judge gates.

16
00:01:19.976 --> 00:01:24.115
For a release gate on harmful replies, TNR is the safety number.

17
00:01:24.395 --> 00:01:30.453
For a dashboard of overall quality, both matter, and in a moment you’ll use both to correct the dashboard.

18
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:43.640
Thakur and colleagues (2024) make the same point from research on judges: judges with high percent agreement can still assign very different scores, so you need metrics beyond raw agreement.
